    "https://jobs-shepherd.peopleadmin.com/postings/18890": {
      "title": "Shepherd University Portal | Director of Assessment",
      "description": "Shepherd University Portal | Director of Assessment Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Director of Assessment Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Information Posting Number S418P Working Title Director of Assessment FLSA Exempt Pay Grade Non-Classified Advertised Salary $72,000-$82,000 Position Status Full Time Appointment Length 12 Months Department Academic Affairs Job Summary/Basic Function Reporting to the Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs, the Director of Assessment provides strategic leadership for the development and sustainability of assessment practices across the University. The Director serves as Shepherd University’s institutional leader for academic and administrative assessment, working collaboratively with faculty, staff, and administrators to cultivate a thriving culture of continuous improvement and evidence-informed decision making. The Director leads institutional efforts to develop meaningful student learning outcomes, implement consistent assessment processes, analyze assessment findings, and facilitate the use of assessment results to improve student learning, academic quality, and enrich the student experience across the University. Minimum Qualifications · Master’s degree from a regionally accredited institution (doctorate preferred). · Five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in higher education involving academic assessment, institutional effectiveness, faculty development, curriculum, or related areas. · Demonstrated knowledge of student learning assessment methodologies and continuous improvement practices in higher education. · Experience designing and implementing assessment plans at the program, college, or institutional level. · Excellent written, verbal, analytical, and presentation skills. · Demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships with faculty, staff, and administrators. · Experience using assessment data to inform planning, decision making, and program improvement. Preferred Qualifications · Earned doctorate from a regionally accredited institution. · Experience leading assessment initiatives at a public university. · Knowledge of Higher Learning Commission expectations regarding assessment and institutional effectiveness. · Experience facilitating faculty workshops and professional development. Familiarity with assessment management software and data visualization tools. · Demonstrated success building an institutional culture focused on evidence-based improvement. · Experience supporting academic program review and curriculum development. Posting Date 08/09/2026 Close Date 09/15/2026 Special Instructions Summary Appointment to this position will be contingent upon a satisfactory background check. We believe in work-life balance and keeping time for things we love outside our work. Shepherd University offers generous employee benefits , including: o 13 paid holidays o 24 days annual leave (vacation) per year o 18 sick days per year and the flexibility to use that time to care for immediate family members o Wide range of health insurance and other benefits o 401(a) retirement savings with 6% contribution match; eligibility to continue health insurance post-retirement, and other retiree perks o Tuition waivers for employees and their dependents Job Duties Description of Job Duties · Provides strategic leadership for student learning assessment and continuous improvement across Academic Affairs. · Collaborates with faculty, department chairs, school directors, and deans to develop meaningful student learning outcomes that align with institutional and disciplinary expectations. · Leads the development and implementation of a comprehensive institutional assessment framework that promotes consistency while respecting disciplinary differences. · Guides academic programs through the complete assessment cycle, including identifying outcomes, selecting assessment measures, collecting evidence, analyzing results, implementing improvements, and evaluating the effectiveness of those improvements. · Promotes a culture of continuous improvement by emphasizing the use of assessment findings (“closing the loop”) to strengthen student learning, curriculum, pedagogy, and academic support services. · Designs and delivers professional development workshops, training sessions, and individual consultations on assessment best practices. · Reviews annual assessment reports and provides constructive feedback to academic programs to improve assessment quality and effectiveness. · Collaborates with deans and faculty leaders to establish institutional expectations, timelines, and reporting processes for assessment activities. · Develops assessment resources, templates, guides, and other tools that support faculty and staff engagement and reduce unnecessary administrative burden. · Analyzes institutional assessment data to identify trends, strengths, opportunities, and emerging needs across academic programs. · Supports academic program review by integrating assessment evidence into program evaluation and continuous improvement processes. · Partners with Institutional Research to identify and utilize institutional data that informs assessment and student success initiatives. · Collaborates with accreditation leadership by providing evidence of student learning assessment and continuous improvement efforts for institutional and specialized accreditation activities. · Maintains awareness of national best practices, emerging trends, and professional standards related to assessment, institutional effectiveness, and student learning. · Serves on institutional committees and task forces related to assessment, academic quality, and continuous improvement. · Performs other duties as assigned by the Provost. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of Professional References Optional Documents Transcripts VISIT 301 N KING ST, SHEPHERDSTOWN, WV  25443 MAIL P.O. BOX 5000, SHEPHERDSTOWN, WV  25443 CALL 304.876.5000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs-shepherd.peopleadmin.com/postings/18931": {
      "title": "Shepherd University Portal",
      "description": "Shepherd University Portal | Student Success Coach - Orientation and New Student Programs Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Student Success Coach - Orientation and New Student Programs Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Information Posting Number S416P Working Title Student Success Coach - Orientation and New Student Programs FLSA Non-Exempt Pay Grade 4 Advertised Salary Beginning at $20.51/hr. Position Status Full Time Appointment Length 12 Months Department Student Success Job Summary/Basic Function The Success Coach – Orientation and New Student Programs supports student success, persistence and engagement through individualized coaching and the coordination of orientation, transition, leadership and student engagement initiatives that support student success and retention. The position assists students in their transition to college by providing guidance, support and access to campus resources while overseeing programs designed to foster belonging, leadership development, student success, and campus involvement. The coach serves as a primary coordinator for orientation and new student programs and collaborates with campus partners to create meaningful student experiences that contribute to retention and degree completion. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s required. Experience in orientation/new student programs, student engagement, leadership development or student affairs programming. Strong organizational and project management skills. Demonstrated ability to coordinate large-scale student programs and events. Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills. Ability to build productive relationships with students, faculty, staff, and campus stakeholders. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree preferred. Demonstrated knowledge of best practices related to student engagement, transition programs, leadership development, retention and student success initiatives. Experience planning, coordinating, and assessing large-scale student programs and events. Experience supervising student employees, peer leaders, or student organizations. Understanding of student development theory, coaching practices, and current trends in higher education. Experience working with diverse student populations including first-year students, student-athletes, transfer students, non-traditional students and students experiencing academic challenges. Posting Date 08/03/2026 Close Date Special Instructions Summary Appointment to this position will be contingent upon a satisfactory background check. We believe in work-life balance and keeping time for things we love outside our work. Shepherd University offers generous employee benefits , including: o 13 paid holidays o 15 days annual leave (vacation) per year o 18 sick days per year and the flexibility to use that time to care for immediate family members o Wide range of health insurance and other benefits o 401(a) retirement savings with 6% contribution match; eligibility to continue health insurance post-retirement, and other retiree perks o Tuition waivers for employees and their dependents Applications received by August 18, 2026 will be given priority consideration. Job Duties Description of Job Duties Success Coach (50%) Support cohort-based support programs including Shepherd Success Academy, academic probation initiatives, student-athlete support programs, and other retention-focused efforts. Maintain an assigned caseload of students and provide individualized coaching focused on student success, goal setting, campus engagement, and persistence. Develop personalized plans for success and assist students in overcoming barriers to academic achievement and degree completion. Utilize knowledge of student success strategies to assist students in developing skills related to time management, study strategies, goal settings, test/exam preparation, and self-advocacy. Monitor and respond to Early Alert referrals related to first-year transition, engagement, attendance, and adjustment concerns for assigned students. Conduct proactive outreach to students experiencing transition, engagement or adjustment challenges and connect them with appropriate campus resources and support services. Develop and facilitate workshops, presentations and educational programming and materials that promote student success and campus engagement towards first-year students. Assist with Student Success Center’s engagement initiatives and campus-wide programming. Develop communication and outreach campaigns designed to increase student participation in orientation, leadership programs and Student Success Center engagement initiatives. Develop, maintain and assess student success resources designed to support student learning, engagement, transition and persistence. Collaborate with faculty, staff, and campus partners to connect students with institutional resources and opportunities. Assist in data collection, assessment, reporting and program evaluation for Student Success Center initiatives. Serve in relevant committees and represent the Student Success Center at campus events and meetings. Support departmental initiatives and perform other duties as assigned. Orientation and New Student Programming (50%) Design, implement, assess and continuously improve large-scale new student programs and initiatives, including Orientation, Shepherd L.E.A.D.S., Rising Leaders and other programs that promote student belonging, engagement, transition and success, utilizing current best practices and emerging trends in higher education. Develop and maintain collaborative partnerships with key campus stakeholders, including but not limited to the New Student Taskforce, Admissions, Academic Affairs, Dining and Hospitality Services, Residence Life and other campus partners to support the planning and implementation of orientation and other engagement programming. Coordinate and facilitate New Student Taskforce meetings, including agenda development, meeting facilitation, documentation of meeting minutes and the identification of emerging trends, opportunities and recommendations related to the new student experience. Develop and coordinate ongoing first-year engagement initiatives throughout the academic year that support student success and retention. Create structured programming that supports student adjustment to college life. Lead planning, coordination and implementation of new student orientation programs including content, schedules, and engagement experiences. Design, implement and facilitate a grant funded program, Rising Leaders and a first-year early arrival immersive program, LE.A.D.S.. Coordinate summer and spring orientation sessions, facilitate convocation and participate in open house info fairs, honors orientation, accepted students day, and other new student onboarding activities. Recruit, train, supervise and evaluate student leaders as applicable. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? (Open Ended Question) Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of Professional References Optional Documents VISIT 301 N KING ST, SHEPHERDSTOWN, WV  25443 MAIL P.O. BOX 5000, SHEPHERDSTOWN, WV  25443 CALL 304.876.5000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
